    "recommendations": "Reducing the density of brush and shrubs directly adjacent to the structure and creating meaningful separation between vegetation and the building perimeter could improve defensible space and reduce opportunities for fire to reach the structure. Where continuous grass cover exists around the building, managing that fuel through mowing or clearing within the immediate structure perimeter may reduce fuel continuity and support suppression operations. Thinning or removing scattered shrubs and brush in the area immediately surrounding the structure would further reduce fuel concentrations and improve conditions for firefighters working near the building. Along the single access route, maintaining vegetation clearance on both sides and overhead could support safe entry and exit during an emergency, as a single ingress and egress point warrants particular attention to ensure it remains passable. Ongoing vegetation management within the immediate structure perimeter, combined with preserving any existing open terrain as a natural fuel break, could improve long-term wildfire resilience for the property."
